{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1545 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e79852102d3e169f75dc6e2a4272935a2dcbe3b5b2e4732e455712c9cf39720e" :edb/db-before-t 1544 :edb/db-after-t 1545 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534857 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:10:53.788Z" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220285 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220286 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220287 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220288 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220289 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441224 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441231 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441239 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441242 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186220284 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441285 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186220284 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441239 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186220290 1078 #edb/ref 17592186220294 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221155 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\} & =\\frac{2(2)(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+{2}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221155 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-5t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{2(2)(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+{2}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221159 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221159 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-7t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221167 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221167 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221167 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{2{t}^{2}+5t{e}^{4t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{2{t}^{2}\\}+L\\{5t{e}^{4t}\\} \\\\  & =2L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+5L\\{t{e}^{4t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$ then $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =2L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+5L\\{t{e}^{4t}\\} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221167 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{2{t}^{2}+5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{2{t}^{2}}+L{5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$ then $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441314 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441320 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441315 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441318 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441316 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441317 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441319 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441321 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221462 1050 1200.0 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221930 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{3t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221930 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221930 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{3t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221930 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221946 1071 "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\}.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221946 1071 "Given that $L{\\cos (ωt)} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}$, find the Laplace transform $L{e^{-4t} \\cdot \\cos (7t)}$." 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221946 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{7}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\} & =\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+4}{(s+4)^{2}+49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221947 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186221947 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $L{e^{-2t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186221947 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left." 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186222476 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to compute Laplace transforms. We now examine how the Laplace transform changes when a function is multiplied by an exponential.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nexists for $s>s_0.$ We want to compute the Laplace transform of $e^{at}f(t).$\n\nStarting from the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,{e}^{at}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t,\n\\qquad s>s_0.\n$$\n\nComparing the two integrals, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n=F(s-a).\n$$\n\nFor this to make sense, we need $s-a>s_0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\ns>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as the **First Shifting Theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}f(t)\\} = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this result to a concrete example." 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186222476 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to compute Laplace transforms. We now examine how the Laplace transform changes when a function is multiplied by an exponential.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nexists for $s>s_0.$ We want to compute the Laplace transform of $e^{at}f(t).$\n\nStarting from the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{at}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,{e}^{at}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t,\n\\qquad s>s_0.\n$$\n\nComparing the two integrals, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n=F(s-a).\n$$\n\nFor this to make sense, we need $s-a>s_0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\ns>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as the **First Shifting Theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}f(t)\\} = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this result to a concrete example." 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186222509 1064 "The **First shifting Theorem** states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nSo, when you multiply a function by $e^{at}$, you do two things:\n\n- *Replace* $s$ with $s-a$ in the known transform $F(s)$.\n\n- *Shift the domain* from $s>s_0$ to $s>s_0+a$.\n\nTo see this in action, consider the following example.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{2t}\\sin(3t)\\}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we identify the base transform:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(3t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the first shifting theorem with $a=2$, which means we replace $s$ by $s-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original transform is valid for $s>0$, the shifted transform is valid for\n\n$$\ns>2.\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates the general pattern: multiplying by $e^{at}$ leads to a simple substitution $s:= s-a$, along with a corresponding shift in the domain." 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186222509 1064 "The **First shifting Theorem** states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nSo, when you multiply a function by $e^{at}$, you do two things:\n\n- *Replace* $s$ with $s-a$ in the known transform $F(s)$.\n\n- *Shift the domain* from $s>s_0$ to $s>s_0+a$.\n\nTo see this in action, consider the following example.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{2t}\\sin(3t)\\}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we identify the base transform:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(3t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the first shifting theorem with $a=2$, which means we replace $s$ by $s-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}(3t)} & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original transform is valid for $s>0$, the shifted transform is valid for\n\n$$\ns>2.\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates the general pattern: multiplying by $e^{at}$ leads to a simple substitution $s:= s-a$, along with a corresponding shift in the domain." 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304947 1071 "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304947 1071 "Given that $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304947 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304947 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304950 1071 "Given that  $L\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304950 1071 "Given that  $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304950 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304950 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304979 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304979 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{5t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304979 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304979 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{5t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304995 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304995 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186304995 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186304995 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+L{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186305000 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186305000 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186305000 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}\\}+L\\{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186305000 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =L{6{e}^{2t}}+L{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186368062 1071 "Consider a function $q(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{q(t)\\}=Q(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186368062 1071 "Consider a function $q(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{q(t)}=Q(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{9t}q(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{9t}q(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 false] [17592186368062