{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{5}\\}=\\frac{5!}{{s}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{7t}\\cdot {t}^{5}\\} & =\\frac{5!}{(s-7)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{(s-7)^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441295 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441315 1069 "q-332174" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441315 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3{e}^{t}+4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}$  for $s>1.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441315 1072 #uuid "4a4eb980-3664-5af2-945a-e7530601aa00" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441315 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{3{e}^{t}+4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{3{e}^{t}\\}+L\\{4{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =3L\\{{e}^{t}\\}+4L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$then$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =3L\\{{e}^{t}\\}+4L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{-3t}\\} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1}+4\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s-1}+\\frac{8}{(s+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>1.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441298 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441300 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441305 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441312 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1069 "q-331797" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1071 "Consider a function $z(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{z(t)\\}=Z(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1072 #uuid "526e692c-07a8-5c67-8d8e-821cf2ff406c" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441316 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-5t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-5t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+5)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+5)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s+5),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+5>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-5.\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441294 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441301 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441304 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441309 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1069 "q-331804" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1071 "Consider a function $n(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{n(t)\\}=N(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1072 #uuid "593b9890-14ef-5ad1-b4d2-54793d0a7bd4" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441317 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-4t}n(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+4)t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $N(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+4)t}\\,n(t)\\,\\text{d}t=N(s+4),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+4>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-4.\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441296 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441318 1069 "q-64307" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441318 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=t({e}^{2t}-1)$ for $s>2.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441318 1072 #uuid "b26511a8-09e9-54a7-8467-ef5beee0c7a0" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441318 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{t({e}^{2t}-1)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}-t\\} \\\\  & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}-L\\{t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}=\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}-L\\{t\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s-1)}{{s}^{2}(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441299 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441306 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441308 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441310 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1069 "q-331765" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1071 "Consider a function $h(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{h(t)\\}=H(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1072 #uuid "b781ac41-d289-50fe-8eb5-284b3b53ff9b" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441319 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{5t}h(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{5t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-5)t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $H(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-5)t}\\,h(t)\\,\\text{d}t=H(s-5),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-5>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+5.\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441313 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441320 1069 "q-331827" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441320 1071 "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {t}^{4}\\}.$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441320 1072 #uuid "b82c8fbc-5979-517c-956f-f5afc90605c9" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441320 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{4}\\}=\\frac{4!}{{s}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {t}^{4}\\} & =\\frac{4!}{(s+3)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{(s+3)^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441297 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441302 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441303 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441311 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1069 "q-331790" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1071 "Consider a function $u(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{u(t)\\}=U(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-2t}u(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-2t}u(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1072 #uuid "e41ff26b-731d-5b74-9b6c-2d6a197328e1" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true] [17592186441321 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-2t}u(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+2)t}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $U(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+2)t}\\,u(t)\\,\\text{d}t=U(s+2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-2.\n$$" 13194139534857 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01504f10-66fd-598a-98b7-004911e742cc" 17592186441213 "answer-05595157-cac2-5680-a2b2-64951fa393e3" 17592186441214 "answer-06e0ebd2-9d85-5fa7-b531-e1c5acd0c35d" 17592186441215 "answer-071f7d36-b5bd-5169-8b91-30b9b8f89740" 17592186441216 "answer-08d8997a-8ad0-53f3-a0a0-7bbe2c9205c9" 17592186441217 "answer-0aa0a33a-1b30-5900-af66-8734c7e0b337" 17592186441218 "answer-111d64d2-13e2-51be-bf85-4c204ca01df3" 17592186441219 "answer-14d1b49c-3f00-5712-9605-0f893cef1bcd" 17592186441220 "answer-177fa7ee-9b47-5b28-b20c-baed96a9d314" 17592186441221 "answer-1a805c4e-533c-516a-9bc6-0bfa840ab980" 17592186441222 "answer-1aca2b4f-d235-5113-8f8a-bd1960e18b92" 17592186441223 "answer-1bc22d0e-6715-5dd0-ab8e-b518e61d3e0e" 17592186441224 "answer-20aee91e-5e1b-55e2-b368-ac8987d045e2" 17592186441225 "answer-226789de